WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.900
Base image choice shapes the final image before application code is added.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:15.960
It affects operating system content, runtime libraries, package inventory, patching cadence, diagnostic tools, image size, compatibility, and production behavior.

3
00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:21.180
Choosing a base image is a design decision, not a cosmetic Dockerfile line.

4
00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:25.620
SDK images are intended for build and development workflows.

5
00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:31.960
They include tooling needed to restore packages, compile projects, run build steps, and publish output.

6
00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:40.540
That tooling is valuable in a build stage, but it should not normally be carried into the final production runtime image for an ASP.NET Core application.

7
00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:46.900
ASP.NET Core runtime images are appropriate for many framework-dependent production applications.

8
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:53.800
They include the runtime support needed to run typical ASP.NET Core workloads without including the full SDK.

9
00:00:53.900 --> 00:01:02.300
Runtime-deps images may fit self-contained applications that package their own runtime and only need a smaller operating system dependency base.

10
00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:12.660
Chiseled or distroless-style images can reduce shipped components by removing shells, package managers, and other operating system content that the application does not need.

11
00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:18.840
They can be useful for reducing image contents, but they also change troubleshooting and compatibility assumptions.

12
00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:27.500
Teams should verify that diagnostics, certificates, globalization, time zones, and native dependencies still work for the application.

13
00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:31.860
Smaller images are useful only when they still support the real workload.

14
00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:42.600
An application may need ICU globalization support, time zone data, root certificates, native graphics or compression libraries, health check behavior, or diagnostic collection.

15
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:47.420
Removing everything without understanding these needs can create fragile production behavior.

16
00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:50.340
Image tags should be chosen deliberately.

17
00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:52.680
Floating tags can move over time.

18
00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:58.780
Version tags can be easier to reason about but still may change depending on publisher behavior.

19
00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.400
Digests identify a specific image content set.

20
00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:10.180
Teams should know whether they are building from a moving label or a pinned artifact and how that choice affects patching and reproducibility.
